#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:374
msgid "``devtool ide-sdk`` configures IDEs and bootstraps SDKs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:376
msgid ""
"The ``devtool ide-sdk`` command can provide an IDE configuration for IDEs"
" when working on the source code of one or more recipes. Depending on the"
" programming language, and the build system used by the recipe, the tools"
" required for cross-development and remote debugging are different. For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:382
msgid "A C/C++ project usually uses CMake or Meson."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:384
msgid "A Python project uses setuptools or one of its successors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:386
msgid "A Rust project uses Cargo."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:388
msgid ""
"Also, the IDE plugins needed for the integration of a build system with "
"the IDE and the corresponding settings are usually specific to these "
"build-systems. To hide all these details from the user, ``devtool ide-"
"sdk`` does two things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:392
msgid ""
"It generates any kind of SDK needed for cross-development and remote "
"debugging of the specified recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:395
msgid ""
"It generates the configuration for the IDE (and the IDE plugins) for "
"using the cross-toolchain and remote debugging tools provided by the SDK "
"directly from the IDE."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:399
msgid ""
"For supported build systems the configurations generated by ``devtool "
"ide-sdk`` combine the advantages of the ``devtool modify`` based workflow"
" (see :ref:`using_devtool`) with the advantages of the simple Environment"
" Setup script based workflow (see :ref:`running_the_ext_sdk_env`) "
"provided by Yocto's SDK or eSDK:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:405
msgid ""
"The source code of the recipe is in the workspace created by ``devtool "
"modify`` or ``devtool add``. Using ``devtool build``, ``devtool build-"
"image``, ``devtool deploy-target`` or ``bitbake`` is possible. Also "
"``devtool ide-sdk`` can be used to update the SDK and the IDE "
"configuration at any time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:412
msgid ""
"``devtool ide-sdk`` aims to support multiple programming languages and "
"multiple IDEs natively. \"Natively\" means that the IDE is configured to "
"call the build tool (e.g. ``cmake`` or ``meson``) directly. This has "
"several advantages. First of all, it is usually much faster to call for "
"example ``cmake`` than ``devtool build``. It also allows to benefit from "
"the very good integration that IDEs like VSCode offer for tools like "
"CMake or GDB."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:421
msgid ""
"However, supporting many programming languages and multiple IDEs is quite"
" an elaborate and constantly evolving thing. Support for IDEs is "
"therefore implemented as plugins. Plugins can also be provided by "
"optional layers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:426
msgid ""
"So much about the introduction to the default mode of ``devtool sdk-ide``"
" which is called the \"modified\" mode because it uses the workspace "
"created by ``devtool modify`` and the per recipe :term:`Sysroots "
"<Sysroot>` of BitBake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:430
msgid ""
"For some recipes and use cases, this default behavior of ``devtool ide-"
"sdk`` with full ``devtool`` and ``bitbake`` integration might not be "
"suitable. To offer full feature parity with the SDK and the eSDK, "
"``devtool ide-sdk`` has a second mode called \"shared\" mode. If "
"``devtool ide-sdk`` is called with the ``--mode=shared`` option, it "
"bootstraps an SDK directly from the BitBake environment, which offers the"
" same Environment Setup script as described in "
":ref:`running_the_ext_sdk_env`. In addition to the (e)SDK installer-based"
" setup, the IDE gets configured to use the shared :term:`Sysroots "
"<Sysroot>` and the tools from the SDK. ``devtool ide-sdk --mode=shared`` "
"is basically a wrapper for the setup of the extensible SDK as described "
"in :ref:`setting_up_ext_sdk_in_build`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:442
msgid ""
"The use of ``devtool ide-sdk`` is an alternative to using one of the SDK "
"installers. ``devtool ide-sdk`` allows the creation of SDKs that offer "
"all the functionality of the SDK and the eSDK installers. Compared to the"
" installers, however, the SDK created with ``devtool ide-sdk`` is much "
"more flexible. For example, it is very easy to change the :term:`MACHINE`"
" in the :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` file, update the layer "
"meta data and then regenerate the SDK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/devtool.rst:450
msgid ""
"Let's take a look at an example of how to use ``devtool ide-sdk`` in each"
" of the two modes:"
msgstr ""

