#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:148
msgid "Additional Implementation Details"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:150
msgid ""
"There are generic implementation details as well as details that are "
"specific to package management systems. Here are implementation details "
"that exist regardless of the package management system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:154
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The typical convention used for the class extension code as used by "
"Multilib assumes that all package names specified in :term:`PACKAGES` "
"that contain ``${PN}`` have ``${PN}`` at the start of the name. When that"
" convention is not followed and ``${PN}`` appears at the middle or the "
"end of a name, problems occur."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:161
msgid ""
"The :term:`TARGET_VENDOR` value under Multilib will be extended to "
"\"-vendormlmultilib\" (e.g. \"-pokymllib32\" for a \"lib32\" Multilib "
"with Poky). The reason for this slightly unwieldy contraction is that any"
" \"-\" characters in the vendor string presently break Autoconf's "
"``config.sub``, and other separators are problematic for different "
"reasons."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:168
msgid "Here are the implementation details for the RPM Package Management System:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:170
msgid ""
"A unique architecture is defined for the Multilib packages, along with "
"creating a unique deploy folder under ``tmp/deploy/rpm`` in the "
":term:`Build Directory`. For example, consider ``lib32`` in a "
"``qemux86-64`` image. The possible architectures in the system are "
"\"all\", \"qemux86_64\", \"lib32:qemux86_64\", and \"lib32:x86\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:176
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The ``${MLPREFIX}`` variable is stripped from ``${PN}`` during RPM "
"packaging. The naming for a normal RPM package and a Multilib RPM package"
" in a ``qemux86-64`` system resolves to something similar to "
"``bash-4.1-r2.x86_64.rpm`` and ``bash-4.1.r2.lib32_x86.rpm``, "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:182
msgid ""
"When installing a Multilib image, the RPM backend first installs the base"
" image and then installs the Multilib libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:185
msgid ""
"The build system relies on RPM to resolve the identical files in the two "
"(or more) Multilib packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:188
msgid "Here are the implementation details for the IPK Package Management System:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:190
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The ``${MLPREFIX}`` is not stripped from ``${PN}`` during IPK packaging. "
"The naming for a normal RPM package and a Multilib IPK package in a "
"``qemux86-64`` system resolves to something like "
"``bash_4.1-r2.x86_64.ipk`` and ``lib32-bash_4.1-rw:x86.ipk``, "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:196
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The IPK deploy folder is not modified with ``${MLPREFIX}`` because "
"packages with and without the Multilib feature can exist in the same "
"folder due to the ``${PN}`` differences."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:200
msgid ""
"IPK defines a sanity check for Multilib installation using certain rules "
"for file comparison, overridden, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:204
msgid "Installing Multiple Versions of the Same Library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:206
msgid ""
"There might be situations where you need to install and use multiple "
"versions of the same library on the same system at the same time. This "
"almost always happens when a library API changes and you have multiple "
"pieces of software that depend on the separate versions of the library. "
"To accommodate these situations, you can install multiple versions of the"
" same library in parallel on the same system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:213
msgid ""
"The process is straightforward as long as the libraries use proper "
"versioning. With properly versioned libraries, all you need to do to "
"individually specify the libraries is create separate, appropriately "
"named recipes where the :term:`PN` part of the name includes a portion "
"that differentiates each library version (e.g. the major part of the "
"version number). Thus, instead of having a single recipe that loads one "
"version of a library (e.g. ``clutter``), you provide multiple recipes "
"that result in different versions of the libraries you want. As an "
"example, the following two recipes would allow the two separate versions "
"of the ``clutter`` library to co-exist on the same system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/libraries.rst:230
msgid ""
"Additionally, if you have other recipes that depend on a given library, "
"you need to use the :term:`DEPENDS` variable to create the dependency. "
"Continuing with the same example, if you want to have a recipe depend on "
"the 1.8 version of the ``clutter`` library, use the following in your "
"recipe::"
