#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:26
msgid "Excluding Packages from an Image"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:28
msgid ""
"You might find it necessary to prevent specific packages from being "
"installed into an image. If so, you can use several variables to direct "
"the build system to essentially ignore installing recommended packages or"
" to not install a package at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:33
msgid ""
"The following list introduces variables you can use to prevent packages "
"from being installed into your image. Each of these variables only works "
"with IPK and RPM package types, not for Debian packages. Also, you can "
"use these variables in a distro :term:`configuration file` or attach them"
" to a specific image recipe by using a recipe name override. For more "
"detail on the variables, see the descriptions in the Yocto Project "
"Reference Manual's glossary chapter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:41
msgid ""
":term:`BAD_RECOMMENDATIONS`: Use this variable to specify \"recommended-"
"only\" packages that you do not want installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:45
msgid ""
":term:`NO_RECOMMENDATIONS`: Use this variable to prevent all "
"\"recommended-only\" packages from being installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:49
msgid ""
":term:`PACKAGE_EXCLUDE`: Use this variable to prevent specific packages "
"from being installed regardless of whether they are \"recommended-only\" "
"or not. You need to realize that the build process could fail with an "
"error when you prevent the installation of a package whose presence is "
"required by an installed package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:57
msgid "Incrementing a Package Version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:59
msgid ""
"This section provides some background on how binary package versioning is"
" accomplished and presents some of the services, variables, and "
"terminology involved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:63
msgid ""
"In order to understand binary package versioning, you need to consider "
"the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:66
msgid ""
"Binary Package: The binary package that is eventually built and installed"
" into an image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:69
msgid ""
"Binary Package Version: The binary package version is composed of two "
"components --- a version and a revision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:74
msgid ""
"Technically, a third component, the \"epoch\" (i.e. :term:`PE`) is "
"involved but this discussion for the most part ignores :term:`PE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:77
msgid ""
"The version and revision are taken from the :term:`PV` and :term:`PR` "
"variables, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:81
msgid ""
":term:`PV`: The recipe version. :term:`PV` represents the version of the "
"software being packaged. Do not confuse :term:`PV` with the binary "
"package version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:85
msgid ":term:`PR`: The recipe revision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:87
msgid ""
":yocto_wiki:`PR Service </PR_Service>`: A network-based service that "
"helps automate keeping package feeds compatible with existing package "
"manager applications such as RPM, APT, and OPKG."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:92
msgid ""
"Whenever the binary package content changes, the binary package version "
"must change. Changing the binary package version is accomplished by "
"changing or \"bumping\" the :term:`PR` and/or :term:`PV` values. "
"Increasing these values occurs one of two ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:97
msgid "Automatically using a Package Revision Service (PR Service)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:99
msgid "Manually incrementing the :term:`PR` and/or :term:`PV` variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:101
msgid ""
"Given a primary challenge of any build system and its users is how to "
"maintain a package feed that is compatible with existing package manager "
"applications such as RPM, APT, and OPKG, using an automated system is "
"much preferred over a manual system. In either system, the main "
"requirement is that binary package version numbering increases in a "
"linear fashion and that there is a number of version components that "
"support that linear progression. For information on how to ensure package"
" revisioning remains linear, see the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/packages:automatically incrementing a package version number`\" "
"section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:112
msgid ""
"The following three sections provide related information on the PR "
"Service, the manual method for \"bumping\" :term:`PR` and/or :term:`PV`, "
"and on how to ensure binary package revisioning remains linear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:117
msgid "Working With a PR Service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:119
msgid ""
"As mentioned, attempting to maintain revision numbers in the "
":term:`Metadata` is error prone, inaccurate, and causes problems for "
"people submitting recipes. Conversely, the PR Service automatically "
"generates increasing numbers, particularly the revision field, which "
"removes the human element."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:127
msgid ""
"For additional information on using a PR Service, you can see the "
":yocto_wiki:`PR Service </PR_Service>` wiki page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:130
msgid ""
"The Yocto Project uses variables in order of decreasing priority to "
"facilitate revision numbering (i.e. :term:`PE`, :term:`PV`, and "
":term:`PR` for epoch, version, and revision, respectively). The values "
"are highly dependent on the policies and procedures of a given "
"distribution and package feed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:138
msgid ""
"Because the OpenEmbedded build system uses \":ref:`signatures <overview-"
"manual/concepts:checksums (signatures)>`\", which are unique to a given "
"build, the build system knows when to rebuild packages. All the inputs "
"into a given task are represented by a signature, which can trigger a "
"rebuild when different. Thus, the build system itself does not rely on "
"the :term:`PR`, :term:`PV`, and :term:`PE` numbers to trigger a rebuild. "
"The signatures, however, can be used to generate these values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:147
msgid ""
"The PR Service works with both ``OEBasic`` and ``OEBasicHash`` "
"generators. The value of :term:`PR` bumps when the checksum changes and "
"the different generator mechanisms change signatures under different "
"circumstances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:152
msgid ""
"As implemented, the build system includes values from the PR Service into"
" the :term:`PR` field as an addition using the form \"``.x``\" so ``r0`` "
"becomes ``r0.1``, ``r0.2`` and so forth. This scheme allows existing "
":term:`PR` values to be used for whatever reasons, which include manual "
":term:`PR` bumps, should it be necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:158
msgid ""
"By default, the PR Service is not enabled or running. Thus, the packages "
"generated are just \"self consistent\". The build system adds and removes"
" packages and there are no guarantees about upgrade paths but images will"
" be consistent and correct with the latest changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:163
msgid ""
"The simplest form for a PR Service is for a single host development "
"system that builds the package feed (building system). For this scenario,"
" you can enable a local PR Service by setting :term:`PRSERV_HOST` in your"
" :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` file in the :term:`Build "
"Directory`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:170
msgid ""
"Once the service is started, packages will automatically get increasing "
":term:`PR` values and BitBake takes care of starting and stopping the "
"server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:174
msgid ""
"If you have a more complex setup where multiple host development systems "
"work against a common, shared package feed, you have a single PR Service "
"running and it is connected to each building system. For this scenario, "
"you need to start the PR Service using the ``bitbake-prserv`` command::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:181
msgid ""
"In addition to hand-starting the service, you need to update the :ref"
":`structure-build-conf-local.conf` file of each building system as "
"described earlier so each system points to the server and port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:187
msgid ""
"It is also recommended you use build history, which adds some sanity "
"checks to binary package versions, in conjunction with the server that is"
" running the PR Service. To enable build history, add the following to "
"each building system's :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` file (or "
"set this in your distro :term:`configuration file`)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:197
msgid ""
"For information on build history, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/build-"
"quality:maintaining build output quality with \\`\\`buildhistory\\`\\``\""
" section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:203
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system does not maintain :term:`PR` information as"
" part of the shared state (sstate) packages. If you maintain an sstate "
"feed, it's expected that either all your building systems that contribute"
" to the sstate feed use a shared PR service, or you do not run a PR "
"service on any of your building systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:209
msgid ""
"That's because if you had multiple machines sharing a PR service but not "
"their sstate feed, you could end up with \"diverging\" hashes for the "
"same output artefacts. When presented to the share PR service, each would"
" be considered as new and would increase the revision number, causing "
"many unnecessary package upgrades."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:215
msgid ""
"For more information on shared state, see the \":ref:`overview-"
"manual/concepts:shared state cache`\" section in the Yocto Project "
"Overview and Concepts Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:220
msgid "Manually Bumping PR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:222
msgid ""
"The alternative to setting up a PR Service is to manually \"bump\" the "
":term:`PR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:225
msgid ""
"If a committed change results in changing the package output, then the "
"value of the :term:`PR` variable needs to be increased (or \"bumped\") as"
" part of that commit. For new recipes you should add the :term:`PR` "
"variable and set its initial value equal to \"r0\", which is the default."
" Even though the default value is \"r0\", the practice of adding it to a "
"new recipe makes it harder to forget to bump the variable when you make "
"changes to the recipe in future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:233
msgid ""
"Usually, version increases occur only to binary packages. However, if for"
" some reason :term:`PV` changes but does not increase, you can increase "
"the :term:`PE` variable (Package Epoch). The :term:`PE` variable defaults"
" to \"0\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../dev-manual/packages.rst:238
msgid ""
"Binary package version numbering strives to follow the `Debian Version "
"Field Policy Guidelines <https://www.debian.org/doc/debian-policy/ch-"
"controlfields.html>`__. These guidelines define how versions are compared"
" and what \"increasing\" a version means."
msgstr ""

